menu "CPE WAN Configuration"

    config CPE_USB_TASK_STACK_SIZE
        int "USB client task stack size"
        default 4096
        range 2048 16384

    config CPE_USB_TASK_PRIORITY
        int "USB client task priority"
        default 10
        range 5 23
        help
            Keep this below the lwIP/Wi-Fi task priorities (18 by default).
            The official example used configMAX_PRIORITIES-1 (24), which could
            starve the network stack.

    config CPE_USB_TASK_CORE_ID
        int "USB task core id (-1 = no affinity)"
        default -1

    config CPE_LINK_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Timeout waiting for the USB 4G link (ms)"
        default 30000
        range 1000 600000
        help
            If no USB link is reported within this time the link manager
            rebuilds the ECM driver and eventually power-cycles the root port.

    config CPE_IP_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Timeout waiting for an IP address after link up (ms)"
        default 30000
        range 10000 300000
        help
            How long the link manager waits for an IP after the 4G link comes
            up before it starts the recovery ladder (DHCP restarts, ECM
            rebuilds, module restart). The module runs the DHCP server on the
            local ECM link, so a healthy modem answers within seconds: field
            logs show an address about 4 s after link up, including right after
            a module restart. 30 s leaves several times that margin while
            keeping the recovery short; raise it if a modem is known to answer
            more slowly. The AP/Web console stays reachable either way.

    config CPE_PDP_WAIT_MS
        int "Time an inactive PDP context is tolerated before the modem is restarted (ms)"
        default 60000
        range 0 600000
        help
            The 4G module runs its own DHCP server on the ECM interface and
            answers it even with no SIM card inserted, so an IP address alone
            does not prove that traffic can flow: what carries the data is the
            PDP context, reported by the AT telemetry (AT+CGACT?). With this
            option the CPE only counts as ONLINE while that context is active,
            and restarts the module when it stays inactive for this long
            (bounded by CPE_IP_MODEM_RESETS, the same budget as the "no IP"
            ladder). The state has to persist for the whole window: a single
            poll reporting the context down while the modem is attaching is not
            a reason to restart it. An unknown PDP state - AT telemetry
            disabled, a modem that does not answer the command, or the first
            seconds before the first poll - counts as usable, so the CPE still
            comes online without AT. 0 disables the check: an IP is enough,
            the behaviour before this option existed.

    config CPE_IP_DHCP_RESTARTS
        int "DHCP-client restarts before rebuilding the ECM driver"
        default 1
        range 0 10
        help
            When the link is up but no IP arrives, keep the ECM/USB session
            alive and only restart the DHCP client this many times first. Each
            attempt costs a full CPE_IP_TIMEOUT_MS, and a modem-side DHCP hang
            (the case seen in the field) is not fixed by it, so one restart is
            enough to clear a wedged client before the driver is rebuilt.
            0 keeps the old behaviour (rebuild ECM on the first timeout).

    config CPE_IP_MAX_ECM_REBUILDS
        int "ECM rebuilds before a modem restart or DHCP-only recovery"
        default 1
        range 0 20
        help
            Bounds the expensive step of the recovery: every ECM rebuild resets
            the modem USB interface, and repeating it never helps when the real
            cause is a modem-side DHCP hang, a missing SIM or an inactive PDP
            context. After this many rebuilds the ladder escalates to restarting
            the module itself (CPE_IP_MODEM_RESETS); once that budget is used up
            it degrades to DHCP retries only, so a SIM (or a PDP context) that
            shows up later is still picked up while the SoftAP/Web console stays
            available. One rebuild is enough to recover a stale driver or netif
            on the ESP side without spending minutes before the module restart.
            The counter is reset when an IP is acquired, when the CPE reaches
            ONLINE and by a scheduled rebuild; it deliberately survives the link
            drop that a rebuild itself causes, otherwise every rebuild would
            start with a fresh budget. 0 keeps the old behaviour (rebuild ECM on
            every timeout, unbounded).

    config CPE_IP_MODEM_RESETS
        int "Modem restarts when the link is up but no IP arrives"
        default 2
        range 0 5
        help
            Last step of the "link up but no IP" ladder, tried only after the
            DHCP-client restarts AND the ECM rebuilds above are both exhausted:
            restarting the 4G module itself (AT+CFUN=1,1). Field observation:
            a modem that keeps its ECM session but never answers DHCP comes back
            with an address after a module restart, while rebuilding the ECM
            driver alone does not. The module drops off the USB bus, so the ECM
            interface is torn down and recreated; the ladder then starts over
            (DHCP -> ECM rebuild -> another module restart) until this budget is
            used up, after which recovery degrades to DHCP retries only. The
            counter is reset when an IP is acquired, when the CPE reaches ONLINE
            and by a scheduled rebuild. Requires AT telemetry
            (CONFIG_CPE_AT_ENABLE): without it the step is skipped and DHCP
            retries continue. 0 disables the step.

endmenu
